(4901211, 710116, 97166, '<td valign="top">Follow up on my comment:<br/> <br/>I was being a little super-picky on my original commenting and voting of this image.  It''s to my own loss that I didn''t spend the time to really feel the image then, but I do now.  The posing of the woody here is, at the expense of sounding like a kiss-ass, ingeniously done.  To the point where I don''t even see a woody now, I <b>see</b> C-3P0.<br/> <br/>While I gave you a respectable 6, I believe had I come back to actually spend some time with this image, I''d have bumped you to an 8.  Consider my adding this photograph as a favorite to be my way of repentance. <br/> <br/>No, it still isn''t the most perfectly technical shot, but to hell with that.</td>', 'Follow up on my comment: I was being a little super-picky on my original commenting and voting of this image.  It''s to my own loss that I didn''t spend the time to really feel the image then, but I do now.  The posing of the woody here is, at the expense of sounding like a kiss-ass, ingeniously done.  To the point where I don''t even see a woody now, I see C-3P0. While I gave you a respectable 6, I believe had I come back to actually spend some time with this image, I''d have bumped you to an 8.  Consider my adding this photograph as a favorite to be my way of repentance.  No, it still isn''t the most perfectly technical shot, but to hell with that.', '2008-08-20 21:23:42.000000', NULL, 0);
INSERT INTO comments ("id", "image_id", "commenter_id", "raw_comment", "comment", "date", "edited", "made_during_challenge") VALUES
(4901219, 709970, 97166, '<td valign="top">Follow up to my comment:<br/> <br/>I''m sorry that I didn''t get the time to come back and be a little more clear on what I meant with my one-liner.<br/> <br/>I''ve seen this type of shot done before, so I was comparing it to the ones I''ve seen.  What I see here is that you''ve not allowed the viewer to really <b>see</b> what is in those droplets by leaving the shot too far out.  I understand that sometimes equipment can be a real roadblock to this sort of thing, but for a shot like this, you really want the viewer to be able to recognize what it is you''re reflecting without squinching up to the screen.<br/> <br/>Focus is also a little off, which some commenters pointed to.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Follow up to my comment: I''m sorry that I didn''t get the time to come back and be a little more clear on what I meant with my one-liner. I''ve seen this type of shot done before, so I was comparing it to the ones I''ve seen.  What I see here is that you''ve not allowed the viewer to really see what is in those droplets by leaving the shot too far out.  I understand that sometimes equipment can be a real roadblock to this sort of thing, but for a shot like this, you really want the viewer to be able to recognize what it is you''re reflecting without squinching up to the screen. Focus is also a little off, which some commenters pointed to. ', '2008-08-20 21:28:24.000000', NULL, 0),
